\section{Atmospheric Descent Dynamics}
\label{sec:atmospheric-descent-dynamics}

\subsection{Overview}
Following hypersonic entry and shock-layer stabilization, the UAP-01
atmospheric probe transitions into a multi-phase descent regime governed
by aerodynamic drag, thermochemical cooling, parachute deployment, and
pressure–temperature stratification. This chapter derives the governing
equations for descent velocity, dynamic pressure, thermal balance, and
terminal science stability within Uranus’s upper and middle atmosphere.

\subsection{Descent Phases}
The descent sequence consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Hypersonic deceleration (post-shock regime).
    \item Supersonic drag-dominated descent.
    \item Parachute deployment and subsonic stabilization.
    \item Terminal descent and science operations.
\end{enumerate}

Each phase is governed by distinct aerodynamic and thermodynamic
conditions.

\subsection{Post-Shock Velocity Decay}
Velocity decay after peak heating is governed by:
\[
\frac{dv}{dt}
= -\frac{1}{2m} \rho(h) v^2 C_D A,
\]
where:
\[
m = \text{probe mass}, \quad
\rho(h) = \text{atmospheric density}, \quad
C_D = \text{drag coefficient}, \quad
A = \text{reference area}.
\]

Atmospheric density follows an exponential profile:
\[
\rho(h) = \rho_0 \exp\left(-\frac{h}{H}\right),
\]
with scale height $H$ determined from UUAS THERMO-Q2.

\subsection{Dynamic Pressure Evolution}
Dynamic pressure:
\[
q = \frac{1}{2} \rho(h) v^2.
\]

Peak dynamic pressure occurs when:
\[
\frac{dq}{dt} = 0
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
\frac{d}{dt}(\rho v^2) = 0.
\]

UUAS atmospheric models predict:
\[
q_{\text{peak}} \approx 18{-}22~\mathrm{kPa}.
\]

\subsection{Thermal Balance During Descent}
Thermal balance after shock-layer detachment:
\[
m c_p \frac{dT}{dt}
= q_{\text{conv}} - q_{\text{rad}} - q_{\text{abl}},
\]
where:
\[
q_{\text{conv}} = \rho^{0.5} v^3 \sqrt{\frac{C_h}{R_n}},
\]
\[
q_{\text{rad}} = \sigma T^4 \epsilon_{\text{eff}},
\]
\[
q_{\text{abl}} = \dot{m}_{\text{abl}} H_{\text{vap}}.
\]

Temperature stabilizes when:
\[
q_{\text{conv}} \approx q_{\text{rad}} + q_{\text{abl}}.
\]

\subsection{Supersonic Drag Regime}
As velocity decreases below Mach $\sim 5$:
\[
C_D = C_{D,\text{supersonic}}(M),
\]
with $C_D$ increasing due to shock detachment.

Velocity evolution:
\[
v(t) = v_0 \exp\left(
-\int \frac{\rho(h) C_D A}{2m} \, dt
\right).
\]

UUAS atmospheric profiles yield:
\[
v_{\text{supersonic}} \approx 2.5{-}3.5~\mathrm{km/s}
\]
before parachute deployment.

\subsection{Parachute Deployment Dynamics}
Deployment occurs when:
\[
q = \frac{1}{2} \rho v^2 < q_{\text{max}},
\]
with:
\[
q_{\text{max}} \approx 8{-}12~\mathrm{kPa}.
\]

Deployment altitude:
\[
h_{\text{deploy}} \approx 250{-}300~\mathrm{km}.
\]

Post-deployment drag force:
\[
D_{\text{chute}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho v^2 C_{D,\text{chute}} A_{\text{chute}},
\]
with:
\[
C_{D,\text{chute}} \approx 1.75{-}2.05.
\]

Velocity rapidly decays to:
\[
v_{\text{subsonic}} \approx 120{-}180~\mathrm{m/s}.
\]

\subsection{Subsonic Stabilization}
Subsonic descent governed by:
\[
m \frac{dv}{dt}
= mg - D_{\text{chute}},
\]
yielding terminal velocity:
\[
v_{\text{term}}
= \sqrt{\frac{2mg}{\rho C_{D,\text{chute}} A_{\text{chute}}}}.
\]

UUAS density profiles predict:
\[
v_{\text{term}} \approx 35{-}55~\mathrm{m/s}.
\]

\subsection{Pressure–Temperature Stratification}
Pressure profile:
\[
P(h) = P_0 \exp\left(-\frac{h}{H_P}\right),
\]
Temperature profile:
\[
T(h) = T_0 + \Gamma h,
\]
where $\Gamma$ is lapse rate derived from UUAS THERMO-Q2.

Probe sensors measure:
\begin{itemize}
    \item methane fraction,
    \item aerosol density,
    \item radiative flux,
    \item wind shear.
\end{itemize}

These data validate deep-mantle EOS and ionospheric coupling models.

\subsection{Terminal Science Operations}
At terminal descent altitude:
\[
h_{\text{science}} \approx 80{-}120~\mathrm{km},
\]
the probe stabilizes for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item composition sampling,
    \item radiative flux measurement,
    \item wind vector reconstruction,
    \item plasma density profiling.
\end{itemize}

Telemetry is encrypted via FHE-Q3 and relayed to the orbiter.

\subsection{Conclusion}
The UAP-01 atmospheric probe undergoes a multi-phase descent governed by
hypersonic deceleration, radiative cooling, parachute stabilization, and
pressure–temperature stratification. Derived descent dynamics confirm
stable terminal operations and full compatibility with UUAS atmospheric,
thermochemical, and plasma-kinetic models.